1
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:10,510
Greetings and welcome to the lecture on administrative control.

2
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:16,430
In this lecture, I will introduce you to the concept of the administrative control

3
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:23,630
which governs the safe working operation of a containment facility. In fact, an administrative

4
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:30,510
control must be established in the form of a laboratory biosafety manual as well as a

5
00:00:30,590 --> 00:00:41,600
biorisk management policy prior to the design and development of your facility. These are learning

6
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:48,240
objectives for today's lecture. Firstly, I will introduce you to the concept of the administrative

7
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:54,640
control. I will introduce you to the different types of administrative controls which are both

8
00:00:54,640 --> 00:01:00,800
internal and external and I will provide you guidance on the development of your unique

9
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:12,100
administrative control for your specific facility. These are your learning outcomes for this

10
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:17,780
particular module. Upon completion of this module, you should demonstrate the ability to describe

11
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:23,780
the various types of administrative controls, understand how administrative controls must be

12
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:29,940
designed and developed within existing legal frameworks and this is based on your national

13
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:37,140
legal requirements. Finally, you will learn how to design and develop a framework for a laboratory

14
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:50,780
biosafety manual. Administrative controls are generally classified as work procedures

15
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:57,180
and these are in the form of written safety policies and I've highlighted this word here written

16
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:04,540
because everything must be documented. Documentation is critical because auditors

17
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:11,660
will definitely want to look at your records. Secondly, they are rules,

18
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:19,980
they comprise schedules, training and documentation. These are the general

19
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:30,850
descriptions for administrative controls. What is the goal of an administrative control or a

20
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:38,690
set of administrative controls? It is primarily to reduce the duration, frequency and severity

21
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:46,690
of exposure to biological agents and biological hazards and this essentially is risk mitigation.

22
00:02:52,080 --> 00:03:00,940
Let us look at the different types of administrative controls. There are internal

23
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:07,100
and external administrative controls. I have used the acronym AC for administrative controls.

24
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:16,380
External controls are requirements not originating within an organization.

25
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:23,980
This may be in the form of the guidelines or the laboratory biosafety manuals from the WHO,

26
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:33,180
the SAND Working Agreement, CWA 15793 or in the form of a legal requirement from your national

27
00:03:33,180 --> 00:03:41,580
jurisdiction. Please be aware of the national guidelines, policies, acts and laws in your

28
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:47,660
respective country prior to developing your administrative controls. These must be researched

29
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:54,780
and studied carefully as we should not contravene these laws and regulations when we develop our

30
00:03:54,780 --> 00:04:01,980
biosafety manuals and our other administrative controls especially in the area of occupational

31
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:09,820
safety and health as well as genetic modification. Now internal controls on the other hand are

32
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:17,260
developed within an organization. They are designed and developed to meet the operational

33
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:24,940
requirements within a particular facility. However, you must contextualize your internal

34
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:34,060
controls within the framework of the external legal requirements. This is critical to ensuring

35
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:44,880
compliance. These are the various types of administrative controls. One is the Biosafety

36
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:51,920
Management Program. The definition of the committee, the committee as in the case of

37
00:04:51,920 --> 00:05:02,960
an institutional biosafety committee, the staff, education and training, risk assessment, practices

38
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:12,020
and techniques. I will discuss this in detail. Administrative controls also encompass safety

39
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:20,020
manuals and SOPs, signs and labeling, registration and inventory control, medical surveillance

40
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:32,780
programs, inspections, audits and certifications and documentation. Administrative controls

41
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:40,140
ensure both conformity and compliance. This is in line with your organizational

42
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:47,340
biorisk management policy. The organization is committed to or shall ensure that all relevant

43
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:54,860
requirements are identified and fulfilled. Now an organization cannot claim that they are not

44
00:05:54,860 --> 00:06:04,620
aware. It is the responsibility of an organization to identify any risks associated with biological

45
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:11,340
agents and mitigate the risk and this ensures that the risks are identified and fulfilled

46
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:19,500
as well as your legal requirements. An organization cannot claim ignorance of legal requirements.

47
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:25,980
These must be well researched and documented with the assistance of a legal officer prior

48
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:32,780
to development of your administrative controls and these may be limited to national, federal

49
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:39,820
and regional or state levels. Some countries may also have provincial, city and local

50
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:46,300
regulatory requirements especially when it comes to the release of sewage or laboratory waste

51
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:58,060
into the public sewage system. A biorisk management program comprises a framework

52
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:05,100
of organizational structures, policies and practices which are supported by management.

53
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:10,300
This is very important. Management must support your biorisk management program

54
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:17,260
and the other word I have highlighted is accountability. Now documentation

55
00:07:17,260 --> 00:07:25,340
and documentary evidence of activities ensures accountability. So these all work together

56
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:31,740
hand in hand. So we have our biosafety or biorisk management policy which is translated

57
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:38,940
into a practice or a laboratory practice and finally these practices are supported by

58
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:51,810
documentation and evidence-based records. Let us look at the administrative controls

59
00:07:51,810 --> 00:07:59,840
pertaining to staff and personnel. Now prior to hiring personnel at your specific laboratory or

60
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:06,640
containment facility they will have to undergo wedding or screening and one of the components

61
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:14,880
which we screen for is education. Educational qualifications are essential parameters which

62
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:22,800
define or describe the relevance of pertinence and competency of the personnel to work

63
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:28,720
in a specific facility. The other aspect is training. So for instance you have a bachelor of

64
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:35,600
science degree in microbiology. You may have also acquired training during the process of

65
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:43,280
your career and this is a essential record. For instance this particular course MOC on

66
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:49,440
biorisk management falls in this category of training. So you can attach this to your CV

67
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:58,720
and augment your CV. The other one is competency. Now competency is demonstrated ability to conduct

68
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:06,800
a specific task and this has to be evaluated by the organization. Prior experience is essential. For

69
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:14,720
instance if you are a microbiologist who have worked previously in a pathogenic testing laboratory

70
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:21,040
or a diagnostic laboratory this experience will definitely help in translating your

71
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:27,600
career goals into biorisk management. And finally we have medical surveillance. Now medical

72
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:34,320
surveillance pertains to the records of your medical conditions. For instance if you have

73
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:41,840
allergies these must be recorded in your staff and personnel records because you may have

74
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:49,200
situations where certain biological agents are more likely to infect you as compared to other

75
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:59,120
personnel based on your allergic profile. And this is why we maintain a health history review.

76
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:05,360
So generally when personnel join the organization they are required to undergo health tests

77
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:11,520
including a baseline serum to identify if they have any antibodies against specific biological

78
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:18,640
agents. You may also be required to undergo immunization prior to working with a biological

79
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:26,080
agent. Some organizations conduct mental screening and aptitude testing. They also look at

80
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:33,200
psychology of the individual and behavioral components. And if you are working with high

81
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:39,920
risk group biological agents security screening is essential and this is generally done in

82
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:46,640
collaboration with your police department or your defense establishments. Each country will

83
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:54,320
have its own procedure for screening the personnel in terms of security clearance. And this may

84
00:10:54,320 --> 00:11:05,120
involve intrusive aspects such as screening of the records of internet usage and things of that

85
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:11,520
nature. So this is an essential component in order to ensure that the personnel are not compromised

86
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:22,140
when it comes to security. Another aspect of administrative controls is education and training.

87
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:31,980
Now laboratory tasks must be assigned based on competency. For instance if someone is very

88
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:39,740
good in microbiology and he or she has a demonstrated ability to utilize for instance

89
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:47,420
microscope. He or she should be assigned to that task rather than assigning them to processing

90
00:11:47,420 --> 00:11:54,540
samples which may require for instance nucleic acid extraction. So this is where your role as

91
00:11:54,540 --> 00:12:00,620
a laboratory manager or a virus manager comes in you must assess competency and assign tasks

92
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:07,420
based on competency. Training should also be based on needs for instance if you install a new

93
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:15,260
equipment at your facility you will definitely be required to train all the personnel with regard

94
00:12:15,260 --> 00:12:21,500
to the SOP for that particular equipment. And training may also be based on tasks for instance

95
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:29,180
if your laboratory is constantly screening microbial samples and you're suddenly required to assess or

96
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:37,260
identify nucleic acid base samples you will have to retrain based on the specific tasks. So you

97
00:12:37,260 --> 00:12:44,620
transition from microbiology to DNA and RNA extraction procedures. Training is also based on

98
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:51,980
situations. So for instance if you are currently we are facing the pandemic so personnel who have

99
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:57,500
not been trained to manage the diagnostics of COVID-19 may have to be trained

100
00:12:57,660 --> 00:13:04,380
when a new situation arises and also legal compliance. For instance if your country develops

101
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:10,780
new regulations with regard to diagnostics and testings all your personnel must be aware of that

102
00:13:10,780 --> 00:13:17,740
particular legal requirement. Now training must be site and situation specific. What I mean by

103
00:13:17,740 --> 00:13:23,500
this is that you must train in your specific facility. Invite a biosafety trainer to your

104
00:13:23,580 --> 00:13:29,820
facility and conduct a training at your facility because each facility has its own specific

105
00:13:29,820 --> 00:13:35,740
requirements and conditions and training at one facility may not ensure that you are competent to

106
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:42,060
work in another facility. Assessment and retraining must be done periodically. So a

107
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:49,820
virus manager assesses the competence of the laboratory workers via bench audits and then

108
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:59,420
bench audits and then recommends new training or retraining. Documentary evidence of training must

109
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:06,540
be maintained for audits and competency must be demonstrated. These are the essential components

110
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:14,700
of training. Now training ensures that your personnel are up to date with current technologies

111
00:14:14,700 --> 00:14:20,380
and it also ensures that they exhibit what is known as a high level of situational awareness

112
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:30,800
of the particular training requirements at your specific facility. Good work practices

113
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:36,160
are another component of administrative controls. So these involve good laboratory practices,

114
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:43,120
aseptic techniques and the code of conduct. Now this code of conduct must be developed at

115
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:54,400
your respective facility. Good work practices also rely on awareness of the individuals to

116
00:14:54,400 --> 00:15:00,320
biological agents. So the laboratory workers must be aware of the risks associated with

117
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:07,440
biological agents as the situation develops, training and observation. Now this observation

118
00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:17,280
can be done via internal audits or via external audits. We now move on to the safety manual.

119
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:29,120
Now a safety manual must be designed and developed based on your specific laboratory

120
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:35,360
or containment facility. A safety manual from one facility cannot be adopted

121
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:46,560
to another facility. It is unique and all biosafety manuals must be designed in compliance with

122
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:53,040
the bi-risk management policy of the organization. So these are the various kinds of manuals

123
00:15:54,320 --> 00:16:00,560
which may be components of the laboratory biosafety manual. Generally when you develop

124
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:06,800
manuals you can refer to templates. I will share these templates in the learning resources

125
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:12,240
and these templates are developed by laboratories. You can reference these templates in order to

126
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:18,960
develop the framework for your specific manual. Now within a laboratory biosafety manual you can

127
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:25,120
have your biosafety manual, animal biosafety manuals if you work with animals, biosecurity

128
00:16:25,120 --> 00:16:31,440
manuals if you are working with high risk group biological agents, incident emergency response

129
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:39,120
manuals and facilities operational manuals. Now incident and emergency response manuals are general

130
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:46,000
generic. However facility operation manuals are very specific as each facility has different sets

131
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:52,720
of electrical controls as well as engineering design elements. Now these must be definitely

132
00:16:53,360 --> 00:17:00,560
designed and developed along with your facility manager or your engineers who design the facility.

133
00:17:06,690 --> 00:17:11,890
We now move on to standard operating procedures. I will discuss this in detail in a separate

134
00:17:11,890 --> 00:17:21,650
module. So an SOP is a set of instructions having the force of a directive. A directive means it must

135
00:17:21,650 --> 00:17:30,270
be complied with covering those features of operations that lend themselves to a definite

136
00:17:30,270 --> 00:17:38,430
or standardized procedure without loss of effectiveness. Now one of the examples of

137
00:17:38,430 --> 00:17:44,990
a standard operating procedure is an airline checklist. I always refer to this in my training

138
00:17:44,990 --> 00:17:54,510
as airlines base their safety on checklists and pilots or airline pilots comply with specific

139
00:17:54,510 --> 00:18:01,310
standard operating procedures in the form of checklist which they check as they undergo

140
00:18:01,310 --> 00:18:06,910
or undertake a specific procedure in the aircraft. It is a good practice to adopt

141
00:18:07,710 --> 00:18:15,950
this particular checklist to your facility as it ensures that there is a reduction in the number of

142
00:18:15,950 --> 00:18:27,070
errors as well as a reduction in the degree of incidence and accidents. Now standard operating

143
00:18:27,070 --> 00:18:33,870
procedures can be effective catalyst to drive performance. Why? It is primarily because when

144
00:18:33,870 --> 00:18:40,990
you are adhering to a written set of instructions you are less likely to stray from the primary

145
00:18:40,990 --> 00:18:46,990
objective and more likely to maintain your effectiveness. For instance if I am working in

146
00:18:46,990 --> 00:18:54,750
a biological safety cabinet I must comply with a specific standard operating procedure

147
00:18:54,750 --> 00:19:02,270
which determines the order of operations. If I did not have a SOP or I tried to recall it

148
00:19:02,270 --> 00:19:08,990
using my mental abilities or cognitive abilities I will definitely make a mistake. This is because

149
00:19:08,990 --> 00:19:16,430
operations in laboratories require an extremely high degree of focus and situational awareness.

150
00:19:16,990 --> 00:19:22,430
So you should focus on the psychomotor tasks which is executing the operation as opposed to

151
00:19:22,990 --> 00:19:29,150
trying to recall a standard operating procedure. In general at my laboratory the standard operating

152
00:19:29,150 --> 00:19:38,830
procedures are pasted or taped onto the instrument itself and we follow through with a checklist.

153
00:19:45,810 --> 00:19:54,450
Signage and labeling is a very important aspect in a biosafety laboratory or a containment facility.

154
00:19:54,450 --> 00:20:00,690
Now a word of caution on signage. Please do not use signage where it is not required. For instance

155
00:20:00,690 --> 00:20:10,130
do not stick a symbol for biohazard on every work bin or every drawer. It will basically lose its

156
00:20:10,130 --> 00:20:18,370
effectiveness. Signage should be used judiciously only in places where it is required or else you

157
00:20:18,370 --> 00:20:24,930
will create too much cognitive overload and laboratory workers will tend to ignore the signage.

158
00:20:28,740 --> 00:20:34,980
Coming down to signage. Signage is important to communicate the potential hazard. As a

159
00:20:34,980 --> 00:20:43,540
biorisk manager communication is essential. You must provide contact information especially of the

160
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:52,460
emergency services. A biohazard warning sign must be provided in areas where immunizations

161
00:20:52,460 --> 00:20:58,780
or the use of respirators is needed for entry and persons at increased risk of infection should

162
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:06,140
not enter facilities. For instance if you are immunocompromised or you are undergoing a medical

163
00:21:06,140 --> 00:21:14,060
treatment or you are expecting a baby you are not required or you should not work in a facility.

164
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:24,900
Inventory control is another aspect of an administrative control.

165
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:34,020
So inventories must be maintained for animals, pathogens, genetically modified organisms

166
00:21:34,740 --> 00:21:41,780
and all of this should be documented in a logbook with access records as well as the location and

167
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:48,260
level of security. For instance if you have your laboratory refrigerator you have a refrigerator

168
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:54,100
in the laboratory. All your pathogens or biological agents which are stored in this

169
00:21:54,100 --> 00:22:00,740
particular freezer or the refrigerator must be documented using a record. This

170
00:22:00,740 --> 00:22:07,940
particular inventory must be maintained at all times and archived so you are aware of the

171
00:22:09,220 --> 00:22:18,100
flow of the biological agent in terms of the usage and this constitutes the inventory control.

172
00:22:22,930 --> 00:22:28,290
Medical surveillance records are very important in any containment facility.

173
00:22:29,490 --> 00:22:36,610
The health of the personnel must be evaluated prior to commencement of operations during

174
00:22:37,490 --> 00:22:42,770
the operation and post operation. So generally if you are working with COVID-19 samples

175
00:22:43,810 --> 00:22:50,690
as a case in point a swab test must be conducted prior to commencement of the laboratory work.

176
00:22:52,130 --> 00:22:58,850
During laboratory work and post laboratory work. This ensures that both the personnel as well as

177
00:22:58,850 --> 00:23:05,730
the samples for the procedures are not compromised. Medical surveillance can be conducted via an

178
00:23:05,730 --> 00:23:10,770
annual physical and this may involve what is termed as baseline serum testing in which

179
00:23:10,770 --> 00:23:18,530
case your serum is tested with the various antibodies representing biological agents to

180
00:23:18,530 --> 00:23:23,970
determine if you have an exposure. For instance if you have an exposure to a specific biological

181
00:23:23,970 --> 00:23:31,170
agent you will definitely have antibodies. Your immune system will elicit antibodies and these

182
00:23:31,170 --> 00:23:38,370
antibodies can be assessed using ELISA test an enzyme linked immunosubmit assay and you will

183
00:23:38,370 --> 00:23:45,570
determine if there was an exposure in your laboratory. Finally we have emergency care

184
00:23:46,530 --> 00:23:54,690
which pertains to the provision of emergency care in the event of an exposure to a biological agent

185
00:23:54,690 --> 00:24:06,500
and this is the responsibility of the employer. Audits inspections and certifications are essential

186
00:24:07,620 --> 00:24:16,580
for the proper administration of a laboratory facility. Audits check that procedures and SOP

187
00:24:16,900 --> 00:24:22,660
followed correctly. Inspections check that facilities are functioning according to design

188
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:28,980
and certification checks that equipment etc are within the required parameters.

189
00:24:31,390 --> 00:24:38,910
Now audits can be both internal and external. It is a good practice to invite an external auditor

190
00:24:38,910 --> 00:24:46,190
to conduct an audit as he or she will be unbiased when it comes to examination of

191
00:24:46,190 --> 00:24:55,470
documentation and evidence. Inspection may be essential in the case of facilities

192
00:24:55,470 --> 00:25:01,950
designed within a specific building code. One of the examples is when a facility is

193
00:25:01,950 --> 00:25:07,070
designed and developed you will definitely have to invite the local authorities which

194
00:25:07,070 --> 00:25:13,230
may include the local health authorities as well as the fire brigade and other

195
00:25:13,310 --> 00:25:25,250
civil service providers in order to inspect your respective facility. Now documentation is essential

196
00:25:25,890 --> 00:25:34,420
and we follow this particular guiding principle. Do what you write and write what you do. Data can

197
00:25:34,420 --> 00:25:40,580
be recorded both electronically and using printed checklists. However when you use electronic

198
00:25:40,580 --> 00:25:49,380
record please ensure that you maintain a backup file and that this backup file is secured. If you

199
00:25:49,380 --> 00:25:55,140
use printed checklists you will have to develop an archiving facility for maintenance of this

200
00:25:55,140 --> 00:26:01,860
checklist. All records must be confirmed and verified by the bi-risk manager or other

201
00:26:01,860 --> 00:26:11,060
competent authority. Logbooks are an essential form of documentation and if you modify standard

202
00:26:11,060 --> 00:26:17,540
operating procedures and checklists please maintain the version number. Please note that SOPs and

203
00:26:17,540 --> 00:26:23,140
checklists are both controlled documents which means that if you modify them you have to put

204
00:26:23,140 --> 00:26:28,900
a version number. You have version number 1.0 which is the original document. You modify line

205
00:26:28,900 --> 00:26:37,380
number 22. You have version 1.1 date of modification as well as the person who modified

206
00:26:37,380 --> 00:26:44,340
the SOP and this is required in order to ensure that your SOPs are maintained

207
00:26:45,700 --> 00:26:53,060
in accordance with changes that you may have to implement over a period of time.

208
00:26:54,340 --> 00:27:01,460
Archiving of documents is essential and this may be required by the law as well as in order to

209
00:27:01,460 --> 00:27:08,020
ensure traceability in the event of an accident or an incident and finally you have access control.

210
00:27:09,060 --> 00:27:15,940
Now generally documents can only be accessed by certain personnel and the SOPs for access are

211
00:27:15,940 --> 00:27:23,220
also defined in the laboratory biosafety manual. No one should be allowed to make copies of

212
00:27:23,220 --> 00:27:37,310
documents without maintenance of the inventory control. We now come to the summary of this

213
00:27:37,310 --> 00:27:42,990
particular lecture on administrative controls. So now we know that administrative controls

214
00:27:44,350 --> 00:27:52,510
are driven by both external guidance in the form of the relevant guidelines as well as internal

215
00:27:52,510 --> 00:27:58,270
policies which are defined by the organizational biarisk management policy. There are different

216
00:27:58,270 --> 00:28:03,790
types of administrative controls as I have described in this lecture. These include

217
00:28:03,790 --> 00:28:13,780
records, documentation, training, personnel as well as signage. They provide the foundation

218
00:28:13,780 --> 00:28:18,900
for continuous quality improvement. For instance if you did not have any documentation or

219
00:28:18,900 --> 00:28:24,740
documentary evidence at your facility there will be no room for improvement. The idea is to set

220
00:28:25,460 --> 00:28:30,580
a goal for improvement and then try and attain that goal and if that goal cannot be attained

221
00:28:30,580 --> 00:28:39,600
you document why it could not be attained. It is advisable to have a specific person or personnel

222
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:46,560
dedicated to documentation at your respective facility. This is because documentation

223
00:28:47,360 --> 00:28:53,760
involves extensive maintenance of records. For instance if you are a laboratory worker

224
00:28:53,760 --> 00:29:00,320
working three hours or six hours at your facility you can record your activity in a

225
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:05,840
document or in a checklist. However when it comes down to archiving this should definitely be done

226
00:29:05,840 --> 00:29:13,200
by another personnel. I recommend that for management practice a specific personnel

227
00:29:13,200 --> 00:29:20,400
be assigned purely to maintain the records and to archive them. And finally you must

228
00:29:20,400 --> 00:29:24,960
maintain your records in order to comply with legal or audit requirements.

229
00:29:26,640 --> 00:29:32,240
Now in this lecture I have provided you with a summary of administrative controls.

230
00:29:33,040 --> 00:29:39,680
It would be a good practice if you could implement this at your facility if you are

231
00:29:39,680 --> 00:29:48,240
working at an existing facility or if you are a student or in your career as a researcher or a

232
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:53,120
laboratory technician it would be a good practice to develop what is known as a scrapbook.

233
00:29:53,120 --> 00:29:59,360
You try and maintain a book and you develop your own virtual policy or your virtual laboratory

234
00:29:59,360 --> 00:30:05,120
manual and this can be a good indicator of your awareness of biosafety and biosecurity

235
00:30:05,120 --> 00:30:11,840
and it can be appended to your curriculum when you apply for a job at a laboratory or a biosafety

236
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:19,200
facility. That brings us to the end of this lecture. In the next lecture I will focus on the standard

237
00:30:19,200 --> 00:30:26,640
operating procedures and I would like your feedback and support. If you have any comments

238
00:30:26,640 --> 00:30:31,520
on the lecture, the way in which it was delivered or the language or the content,

239
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:38,320
please comment in the forums. Thank you very much for participating and I wish you a happy

240
00:30:38,320 --> 00:30:46,800
learning experience.
